an episode 686 from France Dios from W3C.

29
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:31,270
I'm not sure if I pronounced that right.

30
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:44,310
And there's a couple of articles from the ITE accessible learning labs um about accessibility education through learning engineering accessible software. That's a good article.

31
00:02:44,310 --> 00:02:53,430
And a brief survey of um how things are done in continuous integration and automated testing.

32
00:02:53,430 --> 00:02:55,670
So that's something for after to lay the foundation.

33
00:02:55,670 --> 00:03:04,230
I'd like to start with an overview of what accessibility is. So what does accessibility mean?

34
00:03:06,309 --> 00:03:12,630
Accessibility means designing software so people can use it in the way that works for them, not only in the way the developer happened to imagine.

35
00:03:12,630 --> 00:03:22,550
That might mean using touch, keyboard, switch access, voice, screen readers, eye gaze, headtracking, or another access method entirely.

36
00:03:22,550 --> 00:03:30,470
It also means thinking about things like fatigue, speed, error recovery, cognitive load, and whether someone can complete a real task independently.

37
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:35,589
To me, accessibility is not a separate feature or a layer you add at the end.

38
00:03:35,599 --> 00:03:41,589
It is a measure of whether the product can actually be used by the people who need it.

39
00:03:41,599 --> 00:03:43,830
Thanks.

40
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:48,630
So, what assumptions are normally made when creating apps or software?

41
00:03:48,640 --> 00:04:00,630
A lot of software assumes the user can see the screen clearly, touch small targets accurately, move quickly, read comfortably, hear audio, and recover easily from mistakes.

42
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:08,949
It assumes the user has a mouse or direct touch or two hands, or enough fine motor control to hit a small icon first time.

43
00:04:08,949 --> 00:04:15,589
It also assumes the user can react within whatever time the software decides is reasonable.

44
00:04:15,589 --> 00:04:22,790
One example I absolutely hate is a dialogue or warning that times out before I've had a fair chance to read it and respond.

45
00:04:22,790 --> 00:04:27,749
That kind of design assumes speed and motor control that not everyone has.

46
00:04:27,749 --> 00:04:35,430
There are also structural assumptions that focus will not matter, that a custom control does not need a proper role,

47
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:41,990
that a drag gesture is fine as the only way to do something, or that a user can easily recover if they press the wrong thing.

48
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:47,590
More generally, software often assumes the user interacts in the same way as the developer.

49
00:04:47,590 --> 00:04:53,110
Those assumptions are often invisible until someone with different access needs tries to use the product.

50
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:57,990
Can accessibility be bolted on at a later stage?

51
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,950
Sometimes you can improve accessibility later, but it is much harder and usually less effective.

52
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:12,950
If accessibility was tacked on, you know, right away as a user, the labels might technically exist,

53
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:18,230
but the flow feels awkward, focus jumps around, or the feature only works for the simplest case.

54
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:20,629
There is also a legal and business side to this.

55
00:05:20,639 --> 00:05:28,469
Companies sometimes only take accessibility seriously after a complaint, a lawsuit, or a compliance deadline.

56
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:33,270
But by then, they are trying to retrofit accessibility onto design decisions that were already made.

57
00:05:33,270 --> 00:05:38,870
That is more expensive, more stressful, and usually produces a worse experience.

58
00:05:38,870 --> 00:05:48,310
If the structure of the app is wrong, if controls are custom without semantic meaning, or if flows depend on gestures only,

59
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:51,430
you can end up fighting your own design.

60
00:05:51,430 --> 00:05:56,070
Accessibility works best when it is part of the design and engineering process from the start.

61
00:05:56,080 --> 00:05:57,749
Excellent.

62
00:05:57,749 --> 00:06:03,350
I'm going to move us on now to talk about um different ways a user might interact with software.

63
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:07,909
So, what could people use to interact with software?

64
00:06:07,909 --> 00:06:17,030
There's things like eye gaze, touch, your voice if you can talk, keyboard, mouse, and things like switches. Can you take us through that?

65
00:06:17,039 --> 00:06:30,550
People might use touch, mouse, keyboard, voice control, screen readers, switches, eye gaze, head tracking, facial gestures, sip and puff devices, or combinations of those.

66
00:06:30,550 --> 00:06:35,430
The important point is that there is no single default user.

67
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:40,550
Good software should not assume that direct touch or a mouse pointer is the only valid input method.

68
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:44,309
Can you give me an example of good accessibility?

69
00:06:44,319 --> 00:06:52,469
A concrete example of good accessibility is a checkout flow or signup form that works from start to finish without a mouse.

70
00:06:52,469 --> 00:06:55,110
I can press tab and move through the fields in a sensible order.

71
00:06:55,110 --> 00:07:00,390
Each input has a real label, not just placeholder text.

72
00:07:00,390 --> 00:07:08,391
If there is an error, focus moves to the error or the error is announced and I'm told exactly what to fix. Buttons are real buttons.

73
00:07:08,391 --> 00:07:16,710
Links are real links and nothing important is hidden behind hover, drag or a tiny icon with no label.

74
00:07:16,710 --> 00:07:20,790
If there is a timeout, I get enough warning and a way to extend it.

75
00:07:20,790 --> 00:07:23,749
The same idea applies outside the web, too.

76
00:07:23,749 --> 00:07:33,110
A good app exposes its controls to the platform accessibility APIs so a screen reader, switch system, keyboard, or automation tool can understand what is on screen.

77
00:07:33,110 --> 00:07:41,589
I would encourage developers to throw away the mouse for the day and try to use their own product with the keyboard or another access method.

78
00:07:41,589 --> 00:07:44,309
You quickly find out what actually works.

79
00:07:44,319 --> 00:07:50,550
And what are some quick wins for us and developers?

80
00:07:52,469 --> 00:07:55,270
The quick wins are mostly about using the platform properly.

81
00:07:55,270 --> 00:08:03,270
Use native controls where possible because a native button already knows it is a button, [clears throat] can receive focus, can be activated from the keyboard,

82
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:07,990
and exposes useful information to assistive technology.

83
00:08:07,990 --> 00:08:16,469
If you build a custom control, you have to provide the same name, ro state, focus behavior, and keyboard behavior yourself.

84
00:08:16,469 --> 00:08:18,869
Label every interactive element.

85
00:08:18,869 --> 00:08:21,749
Keep the focus order logical.

86
00:08:21,749 --> 00:08:25,671
Make tap targets large enough. Support dynamic text sizes.

87
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:31,189
Do not use color alone to communicate meaning.

88
00:08:31,189 --> 00:08:36,149
Make errors clear and put focus somewhere useful when something goes wrong.

89
00:08:36,149 --> 00:08:40,389
Test the main workflow with only tab, shift tab, enter, and escape.

90
00:08:40,389 --> 00:08:43,269
Then test it with a screen reader.

91
00:08:43,269 --> 00:08:49,430
Then test it with switch style navigation if you can.

92
00:08:49,430 --> 00:08:52,150
A useful rule is do not only test whether each component is accessible.

93
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:55,430
test whether the whole task is accessible.

94
00:08:55,440 --> 00:09:00,550
I have also been experimenting with codec and switchify.

95
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:06,870
Recently, I wrote a harness where Codex looks at a screenshot, reasons about what is on screen,

96
00:09:06,870 --> 00:09:12,630
and chooses which Switchify action to send over ADB as if it only had switches.

97
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:15,910
Some of the results gave me pause.

98
00:09:15,910 --> 00:09:25,590
If the AI could not understand the UX from what was visible on screen, that made me think I should rethink parts of the UX because a real user might have the same uncertainty.

99
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:32,550
That is not a replacement for disabled user testing, but it is a useful way to stress test assumptions.

100
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:37,509
Automated checks are helpful, but they only catch part of the problem.

101
00:09:37,509 --> 00:09:42,310
Real workflows with real users are where you find the issues that matter.

102
00:09:42,310 --> 00:09:46,710
So now I'm going to move us on to talk about the apps that you created.

103
00:09:46,710 --> 00:09:49,670
Owen, you created Say It.

104
00:09:49,670 --> 00:09:51,750
Are you using that to talk to me now or something else?

105
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:55,030
Cuz you're generating this audio, aren't you?

106
00:09:55,040 --> 00:10:00,150
I am not using the original Say It iOS app for this call.

107
00:10:00,150 --> 00:10:07,509
I am using Podium Speak or Podium.xyz, which is a web app I built for delivering longer spoken pieces.

108
00:10:07,509 --> 00:10:13,670
I originally created it for Dr. Claire Malone, a scientist and science communicator with cerebral palsy.

109
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:20,550
Because giving a talk or presentation is a different problem from everyday AAC conversation.

110
00:10:20,550 --> 00:10:23,590
In a conversation, you need speed and flexibility.

111
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:33,750
In a talk, you need reliability, prepared sections, consistent speech, and a way to move through the script without losing your place.

112
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:39,030
Podium Speak is built as a PWA, so the speech can be prepared and the talk can be presented from the browser in a more app-like way.

113
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:48,069
For this podcast setup, the speech output is routed through OBS, so the call hears it as my microphone input rather than picking it up through speakers in the room.

114
00:10:48,079 --> 00:10:50,150
Thank you.

115
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:56,471
So, let's explore Say It and the other app you created called Switchify. How does Say It work?

116
00:10:56,480 --> 00:11:02,150
Say it started as an iOS AAC app, but the current direction is web- based.

117
00:11:02,150 --> 00:11:06,550
It is now built as a Next.js JS progressive web app.

118
00:11:06,550 --> 00:11:10,550
So the user can install it and use it more like an app while still getting the benefits of the web.

119
00:11:10,550 --> 00:11:17,509
The basic idea is that the user types what they want to say and the app speaks it using text to speech.

120
00:11:17,509 --> 00:11:20,069
But the important part is not just calling a speech API.

121
00:11:20,079 --> 00:11:27,430
AAC design is about reducing the number of actions needed to say something useful.

122
00:11:27,430 --> 00:11:38,389
That means saved phrases, fast access to common sentences, categories that make sense to the user, picture support for buttons, and a predictable layout so the user is not searching every time.

123
00:11:38,389 --> 00:11:41,350
It also means thinking about fatigue and conversation timing.

124
00:11:41,350 --> 00:11:47,990
If I have to press 20 things before I can answer, the conversation has already moved on.

125
00:11:47,990 --> 00:11:55,030
The web version gives me more flexibility across devices, and as a PWA, it can be closer to an app while still being easier to update.

126
00:11:55,040 --> 00:12:03,110
The challenge is making that feel reliable enough for communication because this is not just a productivity tool.

127
00:12:03,110 --> 00:12:05,269
For an AAC user, it can be their voice.

128
00:12:05,279 --> 00:12:09,910
How fast can users speak using it?

129
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:16,629
It depends heavily on the person, the access method, and the prediction or phrase tools available.

130
00:12:16,629 --> 00:12:23,269
Typing with direct touch is very different from typing with one switch, eye gaze, headtracking, or scanning.

131
00:12:23,269 --> 00:12:29,590
If someone is using a single switch, every character can involve waiting for the scan to reach the right item.

132
00:12:29,590 --> 00:12:32,949
Selecting it and then doing that again and again.

133
00:12:32,949 --> 00:12:41,430
Saved phrases, prediction, abbreviations, and good layout can make a huge difference because they reduce the number of selections.

134
00:12:41,430 --> 00:12:44,230
But I think words per minute is only one part of the story.

135
00:12:44,230 --> 00:12:47,430
In a real conversation, timing matters.

136
00:12:47,430 --> 00:12:56,949
If there is too much latency or if it takes too long to repair a mistake, the AAC user can be left behind even if the system is technically working.

137
00:12:56,949 --> 00:12:59,430
So I think about speed in terms of participation.

138
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:09,430
Can the person greet someone, answer a question, interrupt politely, make a joke, recover from an error, and keep enough energy to continue?

139
00:13:09,430 --> 00:13:12,710
That is a better measure than just raw typing speed.

140
00:13:12,720 --> 00:13:19,030
Can you give me some examples of of the technical challenges you faced creating say?

141
00:13:19,030 --> 00:13:25,269
For example, did you write native code and was AI around when you built this?

142
00:13:25,279 --> 00:13:30,629
The hard parts were not only speaking text.

143
00:13:30,629 --> 00:13:33,430
The original iOS version of say it was native and written in Swift.

144
00:13:33,430 --> 00:13:39,829
The current version is a TypeScript web app built with Nex.js as a progressive web app.

145
00:13:39,829 --> 00:13:43,110
So the technical problems are different.

146
00:13:43,110 --> 00:13:47,030
Now on iOS, I was working inside Apple's native app model and speech APIs.

147
00:13:47,040 --> 00:14:00,550
On the web, I get more reach across devices, but I also have to deal with browser text to speech, offline behavior, installability, performance, storage, and consistency across devices.

148
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:05,430
Browser speech support is useful, but it is not identical everywhere.

149
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:14,790
So you have to think about voice availability, timing, volume, interruption, and what happens when a browser or device behaves differently.

150
00:14:14,790 --> 00:14:18,310
For AAC, latency matters because a delay changes the rhythm of conversation.

151
00:14:18,320 --> 00:14:23,269
The layout also matters because every extra action costs time and energy.

152
00:14:23,279 --> 00:14:30,470
Saved phrases, prediction, categories, button size, and recovery from mistakes are not just nice UI details.

153
00:14:30,470 --> 00:14:32,870
They affect whether someone can participate.

154
00:14:32,880 --> 00:14:38,150
When I started this, AI was not around in the way it is now.

155
00:14:38,150 --> 00:14:43,750
Today, AI can help with coding, refactoring, testing ideas, and exploring designs.

156
00:14:43,750 --> 00:14:51,829
But the core AAC decisions still have to come from real use because an AI does not know what it feels like to rely on the software as your voice.

157
00:14:51,839 --> 00:14:56,150
And the Switchify app, why did you create it?

158
00:14:56,160 --> 00:15:02,629
Switchify is an Android accessibility service for people who cannot comfortably use a touch screen.

159
00:15:02,629 --> 00:15:09,990
At the surface level, it lets someone control an Android phone or tablet with switches or camerabased facial gestures.

160
00:15:09,990 --> 00:15:21,910
Under the hood, it watches Android accessibility events, reads the accessibility tree, filters the screen down to actionable nodes, and builds a scanning model over those nodes.

161
00:15:21,910 --> 00:15:30,870
If the app exposes good accessibility information, Switchify can use item scanning to move through real UI elements.

162
00:15:30,870 --> 00:15:39,269
If the app does not expose useful nodes, it can fall back to point scan or radar scan where the user chooses a position on the screen instead.

163
00:15:39,279 --> 00:15:45,030
That fallback is important because not every Android app is built well for accessibility.

164
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:57,269
When the user selects something, Switchify can dispatch taps, swipes, long presses, scrolling, system actions like home and back, and more complex gesture patterns.

165
00:15:57,269 --> 00:16:04,870
It also has an internal menu system for things like gestures, scrolling, media control, editing, and device controls.

166
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:11,509
Input can come from physical switches, keyboard style switch events, or camerabased facial gestures.

167
00:16:11,509 --> 00:16:21,910
But internally, those inputs are mapped onto switchify actions such as select, next item, previous item, change direction, or pause.

168
00:16:21,920 --> 00:16:27,350
The newer PC side extends that idea beyond the phone.

169
00:16:27,350 --> 00:16:37,430
The Android app can pair with Switchify PC and send authenticated commands for mouse movement, clicks, scrolling, typing, and window control.

170
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:40,629
So, the same access method can control a Windows computer.

171
00:16:40,639 --> 00:16:47,990
I created Switchify because I wanted switch access that fitted how I actually use technology.

172
00:16:47,990 --> 00:16:55,509
I had been using iOS for about 10 years and I could access a computer through Grid 3, but I wanted more choice and more control on Android.

173
00:16:55,519 --> 00:17:02,310
Android is a powerful platform, but the switch access experience did not feel flexible enough for me.

174
00:17:02,310 --> 00:17:10,870
I wanted something that could scan real UI elements when that worked, fall back to point or radar scanning when it did not, support camera-based gestures,

175
00:17:10,870 --> 00:17:17,429
and let me configure actions around my own movement rather than forcing me into one model.

176
00:17:17,429 --> 00:17:22,789
It started as a personal need, but the more I built it, the more obvious it became that this was not just my problem.

177
00:17:22,799 --> 00:17:29,430
Many users need an access system that adapts to them, not the [clears throat] other way around.

178
00:17:29,430 --> 00:17:32,230
Switchify PC came from the same idea.

179
00:17:32,230 --> 00:17:37,190
If I already have a good access method on Android, why should that stop at the phone?

180
00:17:37,190 --> 00:17:41,190
I wanted the same switches or gestures to control a Windows PC as well.

181
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:44,070
What is it written in?

182
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:48,630
Switchify Android is a native Android app written in Cotlin.

183
00:17:48,630 --> 00:17:51,669
The settings and normal app screens use Jetack compose.

184
00:17:51,679 --> 00:17:56,230
But the core product is not really the settings UI.

185
00:17:56,230 --> 00:17:59,110
It is the accessibility service.

186
00:17:59,110 --> 00:18:05,590
That service is the longunning part that listens to Android accessibility events, reads the accessibility tree,

187
00:18:05,590 --> 00:18:16,630
builds the list of actionable nodes, manages scan modes, draws overlays, handles switch input, and dispatches gestures back into Android.

188
00:18:16,630 --> 00:18:25,190
Internally, there are separate layers for scanning, switch events, access techniques, menus, gesture execution,

189
00:18:25,190 --> 00:18:28,789
camera switches, and PC control.

190
00:18:28,789 --> 00:18:35,750
For example, item scan works from Android accessibility nodes, while point scan and radar scan are more spatial and coordinate based.

191
00:18:35,760 --> 00:18:45,510
Camerabased switches use the Android camera stack, camera X, and face gesture detection, so facial movements can become switch actions.

192
00:18:45,510 --> 00:18:56,310
settings, switch mappings, gesture patterns, and user preferences have to be stored reliably because a broken configuration can make the app hard to use.

193
00:18:56,310 --> 00:19:00,470
Switchify PC is a separate Windows companion written in CS and.NET.

194
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:09,430
It runs in the tray, uses Bluetooth for pairing and reconnect, validates authenticated protocol messages from Android,

195
00:19:09,430 --> 00:19:15,590
and turns those messages into Windows mouse, keyboard, text, media, window, and status actions.

196
00:19:15,590 --> 00:19:22,470
It also has to care about Windows specific things like packaging, code signing, installation under program files,

197
00:19:22,470 --> 00:19:29,350
and UI access because accessibility software sometimes needs to interact with Windows that ordinary apps cannot control.

198
00:19:29,350 --> 00:19:32,070
Could you draw across the screen easily?

199
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:39,510
If you mean the accessibility service UI, the main framework is Android's accessibility service API.

200
00:19:39,510 --> 00:19:41,750
It gives you the special position in the system.

201
00:19:41,750 --> 00:19:49,909
You can observe accessibility events, inspect the active window through accessibility nodes, draw an accessibility overlay,

202
00:19:49,909 --> 00:19:55,110
perform global actions like back or home, and dispatch gestures.

203
00:19:55,110 --> 00:20:00,549
That is a powerful starting point, but it is not a switch access framework by itself.

204
00:20:00,549 --> 00:20:05,990
The API gives you access to the screen structure and a way to place UI above other apps.

205
00:20:05,990 --> 00:20:16,549
Switchify still has to decide what should be highlighted, how scanning moves, what happens when the user selects something, and how overlays stay aligned with a screen that is constantly changing.

206
00:20:16,549 --> 00:20:26,070
So the answer is Android gives you the accessibility service API but the actual access technique, scanning behavior, visual feedback,

207
00:20:26,070 --> 00:20:29,350
menu system and recovery logic all have to be built on top of it.

208
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:34,630
What frameworks were there and what did you have to build from scratch?

209
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:39,669
The main framework was Android's accessibility service API.

210
00:20:39,669 --> 00:20:50,149
It gives you primitives, accessibility events, accessibility note info, global actions, gesture dispatch, and the ability to draw an accessibility overlay.

211
00:20:50,149 --> 00:20:54,070
But it does not give you a ready-made switch access product.

212
00:20:54,070 --> 00:20:56,630
I had to build the access layer on top of those primitives.

213
00:20:56,640 --> 00:21:08,390
the scanning model, the scan tree, highlighting, the menu system, the mapping from switch events to actions, point scan, radar scan, item scan, gesture lock,

214
00:21:08,390 --> 00:21:12,950
gesture repeat, and recovery when the user makes a mistake, or the screen changes underneath them.

215
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:27,029
The platform can tell me what nodes are on screen, but it does not tell me the best order to scan them, how to group them, how to escape from a bad state, or how to make the interaction understandable to someone using one or two switches.

216
00:21:27,029 --> 00:21:29,350
That is the layer Switchify has to provide.

217
00:21:29,360 --> 00:21:35,510
Are there any gotchas with setting it up before using it that you had to work around?

218
00:21:35,520 --> 00:21:42,310
I have tried to make setup as painless as possible because accessibility tools have a bootstrapping problem.

219
00:21:42,310 --> 00:21:46,470
The user may need accessibility before they can easily enable accessibility.

220
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:56,789
On Android, there are still things the platform requires like enabling the accessibility service and granting camera permission if the user wants facial gesture switches.

221
00:21:56,789 --> 00:22:05,270
So, the job is to guide the user through that with as little confusion as possible and then make the service reliable once it is enabled.

222
00:22:05,270 --> 00:22:13,270
The service also has to survive normal Android life cycle behavior, device lock and unlock, app updates, and different manufacturer settings.

223
00:22:13,270 --> 00:22:18,149
For Switchify PC, there is another setup layer.

224
00:22:18,149 --> 00:22:24,710
Pairing over Bluetooth, confirming the device, keeping the PC app running in the tray, and installing it properly.

225
00:22:24,710 --> 00:22:27,110
So, Windows allows the right kind of input automation.

226
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:36,870
Those setup details sound boring, but they matter because if setup is too hard, the accessibility feature might as well not exist for the person who needs it.

227
00:22:36,880 --> 00:22:42,070
Did AI play a role in creating this at all?

228
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:45,029
Yes, a huge role.

229
00:22:45,029 --> 00:22:50,070
I would say AI helped with about 95% of the implementation work on recent switchify development.

230
00:22:50,080 --> 00:22:55,510
That does not mean the product idea came from AI.

231
00:22:55,510 --> 00:23:02,630
The need, the design direction, and the judgment about what is actually usable came from my own experience as a switch user and AAC user.

232
00:23:02,630 --> 00:23:10,310
But AI has been extremely useful for writing code, refactoring, generating tests, exploring edge cases,

233
00:23:10,310 --> 00:23:14,630
and moving faster through Android and Windows implementation details.

234
00:23:14,630 --> 00:23:17,510
I have also been experimenting with AI as a testing tool.

235
00:23:17,510 --> 00:23:22,950
One example is the codeex and switchify harness I built.

236
00:23:22,950 --> 00:23:31,830
It takes a screenshot, asks codeex to reason about what is on screen, and then sends a switchify action over ADB as if the agent only had switches.

237
00:23:31,830 --> 00:23:39,669
That has been useful because when the AI misunderstands the UX, it makes me ask whether the UX is actually clear enough.

238
00:23:39,669 --> 00:23:48,390
I do not see AI as a replacement for disabled user testing, but it can be very powerful when the person directing it understands the access problem deeply.

239
00:23:48,400 --> 00:23:53,669
Let's go into more detail about testing with AI.

240
00:23:53,669 --> 00:23:55,510
Can you take us through what AI is doing here?

241
00:23:55,510 --> 00:23:57,510
You mentioned it takes a screenshot.

242
00:23:57,520 --> 00:24:02,470
The important distinction is that the AI is not directly controlling Android.

243
00:24:02,470 --> 00:24:12,390
I wrote a parent harness that owns the emulator, ADB connection, screenshots, switchify configuration, and every action sent to the device.

244
00:24:12,390 --> 00:24:16,230
The AI is used as the perception and decision-making part of the test.

245
00:24:16,230 --> 00:24:25,190
I start by giving the harness a goal in ordinary language, such as opening YouTube or navigating to a particular control.

246
00:24:25,190 --> 00:24:30,230
The harness builds and installs the latest debug version of Switchify on an Android emulator.

247
00:24:30,230 --> 00:24:37,510
It checks that the package is installed, the accessibility service is enabled, and Switchifyy's private debug bridge is responding.

248
00:24:37,510 --> 00:24:45,590
It then backs up the user's existing Switchify settings and configures a known test profile using manual item scanning.

249
00:24:45,590 --> 00:24:48,390
The test is deliberately limited to an emulator.

250
00:24:48,390 --> 00:24:50,390
It does not run against somebody's real phone.

251
00:24:50,390 --> 00:24:56,549
And when the test finishes, it restores the Switchify settings and switch mappings it backed up.

252
00:24:56,549 --> 00:24:58,710
There is even a separate cleanup process watching the runner.

253
00:24:58,710 --> 00:25:03,430
So the settings can still be restored if the main test process crashes.

254
00:25:03,430 --> 00:25:08,230
Once the environment is ready, the harness takes a real screenshot using ADB.

255
00:25:08,230 --> 00:25:12,789
The screenshot is passed directly to Codeex as an image.

256
00:25:12,789 --> 00:25:17,350
So the model is reasoning from the same visual information available to a user.

257
00:25:17,350 --> 00:25:23,430
I do not give it Android's accessibility tree or let it inspect the interface with UI automator partly because

258
00:25:23,430 --> 00:25:32,549
that would give it information a switch user might not have and partly because Android's automation APIs can interfere with an accessibility service while it is being tested.

259
00:25:32,549 --> 00:25:37,190
The screenshot is supported by a small amount of diagnostic context.

260
00:25:37,190 --> 00:25:43,430
The model is told which application is in the foreground, whether the Switchify accessibility service is enabled.

261
00:25:43,430 --> 00:25:50,549
The screen dimensions, recent relevant log messages, and the sequence of switch actions already performed.

262
00:25:50,549 --> 00:26:01,909
That action history matters because a single screenshot cannot always tell you whether scanning has just started, whether the highlight has moved or whether the system has returned to the same state.

263
00:26:01,909 --> 00:26:04,149
I also give the model a technical operating guide for Switchify.

264
00:26:04,159 --> 00:26:07,669
It explains that scanning is hierarchical.

265
00:26:07,669 --> 00:26:12,789
The highlight might represent a row, a group within that row, or an individual actionable item.

266
00:26:12,799 --> 00:26:18,390
It explains that selecting a row may drill into it rather than activate something immediately.

267
00:26:18,390 --> 00:26:25,750
It describes the primary and secondary highlights, the escape states, keyboard scanning, and the switchify action menu.

268
00:26:25,750 --> 00:26:32,950
Without that context, the model would naturally interpret the screen like a touch interface and assume that selecting something is equivalent to tapping it.

269
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:38,470
For each step, I launch an isolated AI decision process.

270
00:26:38,470 --> 00:26:45,190
It receives the goal, the current screenshot, the operating guide, the diagnostic state, and the recent action history.

271
00:26:45,190 --> 00:26:53,110
It then has to return a strictly structured JSON response containing what it observed, what it thinks should happen next, why it made that decision,

272
00:26:53,110 --> 00:26:58,549
whether the goal has been achieved, and how confident it is.

273
00:26:58,549 --> 00:27:01,110
In the normal test profile, it can choose only five responses.

274
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:07,990
next, previous, select, capture, or stop.

275
00:27:07,990 --> 00:27:15,029
It cannot invent an ADB command, tap a coordinate, swipe the screen, launch an app directly, or use Android back to escape when it gets stuck.

276
00:27:15,029 --> 00:27:21,909
Even if the model returns something outside that list, the parent harness rejects it before anything reaches the device.

277
00:27:21,909 --> 00:27:30,310
When it chooses an action, the harness sends an ADB broadcast to Switchifi's debug testing bridge.

278
00:27:30,310 --> 00:27:37,190
Switchify converts that into its own internal switch action such as move to the next item or select the current item.

279
00:27:37,190 --> 00:27:43,590
That action goes through the same scanning manager and accessibility service logic used by Switchify itself.

280
00:27:43,590 --> 00:27:45,990
So the AI is not pretending to press the touch screen.

281
00:27:45,990 --> 00:27:50,310
It is interacting through Switchifi's switch control pathway.

282
00:27:50,310 --> 00:27:55,751
The harness then captures a new screenshot and the cycle repeats. Observe reason.

283
00:27:55,751 --> 00:27:58,470
Choose one switch action.

284
00:27:58,470 --> 00:28:02,230
execute it and observe the result.

285
00:28:02,230 --> 00:28:09,269
Each step uses a fresh AI process, so it cannot rely on an undocumented private memory of what happened.

286
00:28:09,269 --> 00:28:15,830
Any context it needs must come from the recorded screenshots, state, and action history supplied by the harness.

287
00:28:15,830 --> 00:28:20,470
The model can also report strange behavior as a structured finding.

288
00:28:20,470 --> 00:28:30,230
It records what it expected, what actually happened, the visual evidence, the severity, and even possible areas of the Android code worth investigating.

289
00:28:30,230 --> 00:28:34,389
The complete run contains every screenshot, decision, action, warning,

290
00:28:34,389 --> 00:28:44,070
and finding, followed by a final screenshot and a summary that makes a failed test reproducible instead of leaving me with only the AI got stuck.

291
00:28:44,070 --> 00:28:47,029
What interested me most was where the AI made the wrong assumption.

292
00:28:47,029 --> 00:28:50,789
This is not about claiming that an AI perfectly represents a disabled user.

293
00:28:50,789 --> 00:28:52,870
It does not.

294
00:28:52,870 --> 00:29:01,029
But when it repeatedly misunderstands whether a highlight is actionable, whether selecting has moved down a level, whether a menu is part of the expected workflow,

295
00:29:01,029 --> 00:29:05,909
or whether anything happened after a switch press, that gives me pause.

296
00:29:05,909 --> 00:29:08,389
The model has the instructions and the visual evidence.

297
00:29:08,399 --> 00:29:12,389
Yet, the interface is still communicating something ambiguous.

298
00:29:12,389 --> 00:29:17,669
That makes the AI useful as a deliberately unfamiliar user.

299
00:29:17,669 --> 00:29:23,909
Its mistakes can expose places where I have relied on knowledge that exists in my own head because I designed the system.

300
00:29:23,909 --> 00:29:35,510
The result is not automatically proof of a user experience problem, but it gives me a precise point to investigate with real switch users rather than assuming the interface is obvious simply because it is obvious to me.

301
00:29:35,520 --> 00:29:40,470
Are you mainly writing prompts or are there accessibility targeted tools you're using?

302
00:29:40,480 --> 00:29:43,350
Environment is everything.

303
00:29:43,350 --> 00:29:51,590
I am using a generalpurpose AI model rather than a specialized accessibility testing model, but I have built an accessibility specific environment around it.

304
00:29:51,590 --> 00:29:55,909
The prompt is only one part of that environment.

305
00:29:55,909 --> 00:30:07,669
You can write an enormous prompt explaining switch access, but if the agent still has access to a mouse, touchscreen coordinates, or unrestricted ADB commands, it will naturally take the easiest route.

306
00:30:07,669 --> 00:30:14,149
It might complete the task, but it will not have tested the experience that a Switch user actually has.

307
00:30:14,149 --> 00:30:17,029
A successful result would therefore tell me very little about Switchify.

308
00:30:17,029 --> 00:30:23,029
The important work was deciding what the agent could see and what it was allowed to do.

309
00:30:23,029 --> 00:30:25,029
It sees a screenshot and a limited amount of device state.

310
00:30:25,029 --> 00:30:31,669
It receives a Switchify operating guide explaining scanning, highlight levels, menus, and escape behavior.

311
00:30:31,669 --> 00:30:39,110
Most importantly, it can normally choose only next, previous, select, capture, or stop.

312
00:30:39,110 --> 00:30:41,830
The parent harness rejects anything outside those actions.

313
00:30:41,830 --> 00:30:45,990
So, I am not merely asking the model to imagine that it uses switches.

314
00:30:45,990 --> 00:30:48,310
I have removed the alternatives.

315
00:30:48,310 --> 00:30:51,990
The environment makes switch access the only available way to progress.

316
00:30:52,000 --> 00:31:00,789
It is the AI equivalent of telling a developer to put the mouse away for the day, except the harness can actually enforce it.

317
00:31:00,789 --> 00:31:07,669
There are accessibility specific components involved, but they are components I created around the general AIS,

318
00:31:07,669 --> 00:31:14,549
switchifi's debug bridge, the manual scanning test profile, the action restrictions, the operating guide, and the evidence system.

319
00:31:14,549 --> 00:31:17,350
The model itself is general purpose.

320
00:31:17,350 --> 00:31:21,350
The accessibility knowledge and constraints come from the environment.

321
00:31:21,350 --> 00:31:23,990
That distinction matters beyond this project.

322
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:27,990
Prompting can influence what an agent says it is doing.

323
00:31:27,990 --> 00:31:30,549
But the environment determines what it can really do.

324
00:31:30,549 --> 00:31:40,230
If we want meaningful accessibility testing from AI, we need accessible test environments, representative input constraints, and evidence of the route taken.

325
00:31:40,230 --> 00:31:45,830
Otherwise, the agent may reach the right destination through a pathway the actual user does not have.

326
00:31:45,840 --> 00:31:51,110
Are there any accessibility challenges using AI and agents?

327
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:56,070
Honestly, the biggest accessibility challenge is very simple.

328
00:31:56,070 --> 00:31:58,789
I wish I could speak to the agent.

329
00:31:58,789 --> 00:32:01,190
Working with AI is supposed to feel conversational.

330
00:32:01,190 --> 00:32:11,029
A speaking user can explain an idea, interrupt when the agent misunderstands, add a missing detail, and redirect it almost immediately.

331
00:32:11,029 --> 00:32:15,269
As an AAC user, every one of those interventions takes more work.

332
00:32:15,269 --> 00:32:23,350
I have to compose the message, navigate the interface, and make sure the agent has not moved on or opened something temporary while I'm still preparing my response.

333
00:32:23,350 --> 00:32:33,350
The irony is that the AI can generate and modify code extremely quickly, but communicating my intention to it can still be the slowest part of the process.

334
00:32:33,350 --> 00:32:39,830
The technology has accelerated the computer side of the conversation without necessarily making the human side equally accessible.

335
00:32:39,830 --> 00:32:44,389
Most AI products seem to assume one of two users.

336
00:32:44,389 --> 00:32:49,909
somebody typing quickly on a keyboard or somebody speaking naturally into a microphone.

337
00:32:49,909 --> 00:32:52,149
AAC sits awkwardly between those two models.

338
00:32:52,149 --> 00:32:58,630
My communication is textbased, but that does not mean it should be treated like ordinary keyboard input.

339
00:32:58,630 --> 00:33:06,070
I may be using switches, scanning, predictive text, or generated speech, and each turn can take considerably longer.

340
00:33:06,070 --> 00:33:08,389
That is why timeouts are particularly bad.

341
00:33:08,389 --> 00:33:12,549
A temporary dialogue might be a minor irritation for somebody using a mouse.

342
00:33:12,559 --> 00:33:17,590
But for a switch or AAC user, it can make an interaction impossible.

343
00:33:17,590 --> 00:33:23,909
By the time I navigate to it or compose a response, it may already have disappeared.

344
00:33:23,909 --> 00:33:31,509
If a decision requires my input, the agent should pause and wait indefinitely rather than assuming silence means I am not there.

345
00:33:31,509 --> 00:33:36,870
A better agent interface would understand that communication speed is not the same thing as understanding.

346
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:47,990
It would let me pause the agent while I compose, cue additional instructions, interrupt using an accessible switch action, and respond to prompts without a countdown.

347
00:33:47,990 --> 00:33:55,110
It would also present its current status in a form that works with scanning and AAC rather than relying on rapidly changing visual messages.

348
00:33:55,110 --> 00:34:01,750
So, when I say I wish I could speak, I do not simply mean adding another voice control button.

349
00:34:01,750 --> 00:34:06,630
I mean, I want the same immediacy in the conversation that speaking users already have.

350
00:34:06,630 --> 00:34:16,310
AI has enormous potential for disabled developers, but the interaction model must recognize different ways of communicating instead of treating speech and fast typing as the defaults.

351
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:19,990
Have you found there's a lot of context switching when using AI?

352
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:28,149
Agents are getting better at maintaining context and I tend to use massive threads rather than constantly starting again.

353
00:34:28,149 --> 00:34:31,030
A long thread becomes a kind of working memory.

354
00:34:31,030 --> 00:34:38,629
It contains the original idea, the decisions we made, corrections I gave the agent and the reasons behind them.

355
00:34:38,629 --> 00:34:49,990
I also plan before asking the agent to implement something that reduces context switching because I am not making every design decision reactively while the agent is already changing files.

356
00:34:49,990 --> 00:34:58,069
I try to establish the goal, constraints, and likely stages first, then let the agent work through them.

357
00:34:58,069 --> 00:35:02,550
This matters more to me as an AAC user because changing context is not just mentally disruptive.

358
00:35:02,560 --> 00:35:09,270
Every correction, repeated explanation, and new instruction has a communication cost.

359
00:35:09,270 --> 00:35:15,589
If an agent forgets why a decision was made, reconstructing that context may take me considerably longer than it would take a speaking user.

360
00:35:15,589 --> 00:35:17,910
There is still a limit to massive threads.

361
00:35:17,910 --> 00:35:24,150
They can accumulate outdated assumptions and the agent may give too much weight to something said much earlier.

362
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:26,790
So planning is important.

363
00:35:26,790 --> 00:35:35,750
The thread needs a clear direction and major decisions should be written down in the repository or another durable source rather than existing only in the conversation.

364
00:35:35,760 --> 00:35:42,950
Overall, context switching is improving as agents become more capable of carrying a task from beginning to end.

365
00:35:42,960 --> 00:35:51,109
My preference is to give an agent a substantial piece of work, keep the context together, and intervene only where my judgment is genuinely needed.

366
00:35:51,119 --> 00:35:55,910
The best agent experience is not one where I supervise every click.

367
00:35:55,910 --> 00:35:58,390
It is one where I establish intent.

368
00:35:58,390 --> 00:36:04,870
The agent maintains that intent across the work and it returns with evidence of what it actually did.

369
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:08,630
What could be done to make this experience better?

370
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:15,109
The biggest improvement for me would be better control over the agent while it is working.

371
00:36:15,109 --> 00:36:19,589
Agents can now do much larger pieces of work independently, which is useful.

372
00:36:19,589 --> 00:36:24,069
But the controls around that work still assume somebody can react quickly with a mouse or keyboard.

373
00:36:24,079 --> 00:36:33,270
I need a reliable way to pause an agent, add information, redirect it, and then let it continue without losing its progress.

374
00:36:33,270 --> 00:36:35,670
Pausing should freeze the task in a known state.

375
00:36:35,670 --> 00:36:41,510
It should not cancel the work, discard the context, or leave files half-changed.

376
00:36:41,510 --> 00:36:45,829
When I resume, the agent should briefly confirm what it was doing.

377
00:36:45,829 --> 00:36:48,069
Incorporate my new instruction and continue from that point.

378
00:36:48,079 --> 00:36:51,030
Interrupting is especially important.

379
00:36:51,030 --> 00:36:55,990
A speaking user can often stop someone mid-sentence as soon as they notice a misunderstanding.

380
00:36:56,000 --> 00:37:03,349
As an AAC user, I may recognize the problem immediately, but need time to compose the correction.

381
00:37:03,349 --> 00:37:08,950
By the time I send it, the agent may have made several more decisions based on the original misunderstanding.

382
00:37:08,960 --> 00:37:20,550
There should be an accessible hold action that stops new work immediately while still giving me unlimited time to explain why those controls must be properly keyboard and switch accessible.

383
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:28,390
Tab and enter are your best friends, but only if focus order is predictable, and every control can actually be reached.

384
00:37:28,400 --> 00:37:36,550
Pause, stop, resume, and redirect should have stable positions, clear labels, and configurable switch actions.

385
00:37:36,550 --> 00:37:41,349
They should not appear temporarily, move around the screen, or disappear behind a dialogue.

386
00:37:41,359 --> 00:37:47,030
There also needs to be a distinction between pausing, stopping, and cancelling.

387
00:37:47,030 --> 00:37:49,910
Pausing means keep everything and wait.

388
00:37:49,910 --> 00:37:54,230
Stopping means finish the current safe operation and return control to me.

389
00:37:54,230 --> 00:37:56,390
Cancelling means abandon the task.

390
00:37:56,390 --> 00:37:59,109
And because that can lose work, it should be deliberate.

391
00:37:59,109 --> 00:38:05,349
At the moment, interfaces sometimes blur those actions together, which makes intervention risky.

392
00:38:05,349 --> 00:38:10,310
The agent should also recognize messages arriving while it is working.

393
00:38:10,310 --> 00:38:14,710
If I send a correction, it should not sit ignored until the entire original task has finished.

394
00:38:14,710 --> 00:38:22,790
The agent should reach a safe stopping point, read the new instruction, and decide whether it changes the current work.

395
00:38:22,790 --> 00:38:26,950
For an AAC user, the message itself should be treated as the interruption mechanism.

396
00:38:26,950 --> 00:38:31,349
I should not have to race to press a separate button before typing it.

397
00:38:31,349 --> 00:38:34,310
This is ultimately about preserving agency.

398
00:38:34,310 --> 00:38:40,870
I want the AI to work independently, but independence should not mean that it becomes difficult to stop.

399
00:38:40,870 --> 00:38:50,950
The ideal system gives the agent enough freedom to complete substantial work while ensuring that I can regain control through one reliable accessible action at any moment.

400
00:38:50,960 --> 00:38:57,109
Is there a better way to train AI tools regarding accessibility?

401
00:38:59,109 --> 00:39:08,230
I think a better approach is to train and evaluate AI in constrained environments where accessibility is not just described in a prompt but enforced by the tools available.

402
00:39:08,230 --> 00:39:16,950
If you tell an agent to behave like a switch user but still give it a mouse touchcreen coordinates or unrestricted automation, it will usually take the shortest route.

403
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:23,030
That proves it can complete the task but it does not prove that the accessible route works.

404
00:39:23,030 --> 00:39:27,910
The agent needs an environment in which next, previous, and select are genuinely its only controls.

405
00:39:27,920 --> 00:39:31,990
That is what I tried to create with the Switchify testing harness.

406
00:39:31,990 --> 00:39:38,630
The model sees the screen, receives enough context to understand the scanning system, and chooses one switch action at a time.

407
00:39:38,630 --> 00:39:42,790
The surrounding software enforces those restrictions.

408
00:39:42,790 --> 00:39:47,589
It cannot bypass a difficult menu by tapping directly on the target.

409
00:39:47,589 --> 00:39:51,750
If it becomes stuck, that difficulty becomes part of the test result.

410
00:39:51,750 --> 00:39:54,630
This principle can be applied to other access methods.

411
00:39:54,630 --> 00:39:58,870
An agent testing keyboard accessibility should be restricted to keyboard input.

412
00:39:58,870 --> 00:40:09,829
A screen reader test agent should primarily receive the semantic information and announcements available to a screen reader user rather than a perfect visual representation of the page.

413
00:40:09,829 --> 00:40:16,550
Any gaze simulation should account for target size, dwell time, and the difficulty of precise selection.

414
00:40:16,550 --> 00:40:22,069
The environment should represent the capabilities and limitations of the input method being tested.

415
00:40:22,069 --> 00:40:25,030
I would still be careful with the word training.

416
00:40:25,030 --> 00:40:35,430
An AI operating under switch constraints does not become a switch user and it cannot reproduce fatigue, involuntary movement, communication effort, or somebody's lived experience.

417
00:40:35,430 --> 00:40:38,069
The aim is not to replace testing by disabled people.

418
00:40:38,069 --> 00:40:48,630
It is to expose basic failures and ambiguous interactions earlier before asking a person to spend their time finding problems that automated testing could have caught.

419
00:40:48,630 --> 00:40:51,270
The results should also be interpreted properly.

420
00:40:51,270 --> 00:40:56,630
If an agent fails, that does not automatically mean the interface is inaccessible.

421
00:40:56,640 --> 00:41:03,190
The model may have misunderstood the screen, but that misunderstanding is useful evidence.

422
00:41:03,190 --> 00:41:14,390
We can inspect the screenshots and action history, ask why the state was unclear, improve either the interface or the agents accessibility model, and then test again.

423
00:41:14,390 --> 00:41:19,030
So, the better way to teach AI about accessibility is through experience shaped by real constraints.

424
00:41:19,040 --> 00:41:25,589
Documentation explains the rules, but the environment determines whether the agent must follow them.

425
00:41:25,589 --> 00:41:31,030
Accessibility should become part of the agents test architecture, not a paragraph added to the end of its prompt.

426
00:41:31,040 --> 00:41:35,270
How did you create Switchify without having it in the first place?

427
00:41:35,270 --> 00:41:43,829
For example, um how do you program or control your ID, etc. And what's that like as a user?

428
00:41:43,829 --> 00:41:46,470
I had been using iOS for about 10 years.

429
00:41:46,470 --> 00:41:51,670
And for desktop access, I was using grid 3 right up until a few days ago when I released Switchify PC.

430
00:41:51,680 --> 00:41:58,870
So, I used the access tools I already had, mainly grid 3, to control the computer and build the first versions.

431
00:41:58,880 --> 00:42:04,790
The answer is slowly and with a lot of patience.

432
00:42:04,790 --> 00:42:09,430
I was using existing assistive technology to build a new assist of technology tool.

433
00:42:09,430 --> 00:42:16,309
In a way, that also showed why Switchify needed to exist because the process made the barriers very obvious.

434
00:42:16,319 --> 00:42:21,109
Are there any differences between iOS, Android, or Windows you've had to handle?

435
00:42:21,119 --> 00:42:24,230
Yes.

436
00:42:24,230 --> 00:42:31,270
Each platform exposes accessibility and input APIs differently, but one of the biggest differences is that touch is fundamentally different from a mouse.

437
00:42:31,270 --> 00:42:39,030
On Android and iOS, you are often trying to simulate direct interaction with a touch screen.

438
00:42:39,030 --> 00:42:43,829
Taps, swipes, long presses, multi-touch gestures, and screen positions.

439
00:42:43,829 --> 00:42:50,790
On Windows, control is built around a pointer, clicks, keyboard input, windows, focus, hover states, drag operations,

440
00:42:50,790 --> 00:42:55,990
and desktop UI conventions that changes the design of the access system.

441
00:42:55,990 --> 00:43:00,470
A touch gesture is usually a bounded action at a point or along a path.

442
00:43:00,470 --> 00:43:11,910
A mouse interaction can involve moving through empty space, hovering before clicking, dragging between windows, using the wheel, or relying on focus in a way that mobile apps often do not.

443
00:43:11,910 --> 00:43:20,950
iOS has strong built-in accessibility features and consistency, but third party developers have less freedom to build deep access tools.

444
00:43:20,950 --> 00:43:31,349
Android gives more flexibility through accessibility services and gesture dispatch, but device differences, OEM behavior, and the quality of app accessibility trees can be challenging.

445
00:43:31,349 --> 00:43:39,990
Windows gives a lot of power for desktop control, but the range of apps and UI technologies is much wider, so consistency becomes the hard problem.

446
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:43,589
What can each OS learn from one another?

447
00:43:43,599 --> 00:43:49,349
Android can learn from iOS's consistency and polish and built-in accessibility.

448
00:43:49,359 --> 00:43:58,790
One of the strengths of iOS is that the system accessibility features feel integrated and predictable across a lot of apps.

449
00:43:58,790 --> 00:44:03,750
iOS can learn from Android's openness and willingness to let developers build deeper access tools.

450
00:44:03,760 --> 00:44:13,349
Android accessibility services give developers more room to experiment with things like scanning, gesture dispatch, and alternative input.

451
00:44:13,349 --> 00:44:18,309
And that openness is important because not every user fits the built-in model.

452
00:44:18,309 --> 00:44:24,630
Windows can learn from mobile platforms about simpler, more predictable accessibility experiences.

453
00:44:24,630 --> 00:44:29,829
But mobile platforms can also learn from Windows about the value of automation APIs and keyboard access.

454
00:44:29,839 --> 00:44:32,150
across all of them.

455
00:44:32,150 --> 00:44:37,670
The biggest lesson is that platform vendors should treat accessibility APIs as core infrastructure.

456
00:44:37,680 --> 00:44:49,190
If an update breaks focus, changes gesture behavior, or makes UI information harder to inspect, that is not a small regression for assistive technology users.

457
00:44:49,190 --> 00:44:59,589
All of them can do better at involving disabled users earlier, documenting APIs clearly and giving developers stable ways to understand and control the interface.

458
00:44:59,599 --> 00:45:05,670
We're now on to the final part of our show where we're going to talk about how we can help others.

459
00:45:05,670 --> 00:45:09,349
So, what can developers do to improve accessibility?

460
00:45:09,359 --> 00:45:16,309
Start by treating accessibility as a normal engineering requirement, not a polishing task.

461
00:45:16,309 --> 00:45:22,710
Use semantic UI components so the platform can tell assist of technology what something is and what it does.

462
00:45:22,710 --> 00:45:25,270
Keep a logical focus order.

463
00:45:25,270 --> 00:45:28,470
Make sure every control has a name, role, and state.

464
00:45:28,470 --> 00:45:38,710
support keyboard and assisted technology navigation, respect user settings like text size and contrast, and test with screen readers and switch control.

465
00:45:38,710 --> 00:45:41,109
Tab and enter are your best friends.

466
00:45:41,109 --> 00:45:47,510
If you cannot get through the main workflow with those keys, that is a sign the structure of the app needs work.

467
00:45:47,510 --> 00:45:52,390
Automated accessibility checks are useful, but they mostly catch obvious problems.

468
00:45:52,390 --> 00:46:01,109
They will not tell you whether the workflow is exhausting, whether an error is recoverable, or whether the app makes sense with an indirect access method.

469
00:46:01,119 --> 00:46:07,190
Most importantly, do not wait until the end of a project to ask whether it is accessible.

470
00:46:07,200 --> 00:46:09,109
What can vendors do?

471
00:46:09,119 --> 00:46:16,630
Vendors can provide better platform APIs, better documentation, and better testing tools.

472
00:46:16,630 --> 00:46:19,829
A good example is gesture emulation on Android.

473
00:46:19,829 --> 00:46:25,670
It took Android 7 versions to add support for accessibility services to perform gestures.

474
00:46:25,670 --> 00:46:32,950
And until the platform exposes an API like that, developers cannot build certain kinds of access tools properly.

475
00:46:32,950 --> 00:46:36,470
Platform vendors decide what is possible.

476
00:46:36,470 --> 00:46:44,230
If an API does not exist or if it is unreliable, every assistive technology developer has to work around that limitation and users pay the price.

477
00:46:44,240 --> 00:46:48,309
Vendors should make accessibility features reliable across updates.

478
00:46:48,319 --> 00:46:52,710
Provide stable ways to inspect UI structure and perform user actions.

479
00:46:52,720 --> 00:47:01,349
Avoid breaking assistive technology and involve disabled users and specialist developers before design decisions are locked in.

480
00:47:01,349 --> 00:47:04,790
Accessibility should be part of product quality, not a compliance checkbox.

481
00:47:04,800 --> 00:47:07,430
Where can developers go to learn more?

482
00:47:07,440 --> 00:47:15,430
The W3C web content accessibility guidelines are a good foundation for web work.

483
00:47:15,430 --> 00:47:18,309
Apple, Google, and Microsoft all have accessibility developer documentation.

484
00:47:18,309 --> 00:47:26,309
[clears throat] Developers should also learn from disabled creators, AAC users, screen reader users, and switch users.

485
00:47:26,309 --> 00:47:33,030
The best learning happens when you test real workflows with people who use these technologies every day.

486
00:47:33,040 --> 00:47:36,710
Right, we better start wrapping up the show.

487
00:47:36,710 --> 00:47:45,109
Um, I've really enjoyed chatting with you about accessibility, but if there was one thing a software engineer should remember from our show, what would you like that to be, Owen?

488
00:47:45,109 --> 00:47:51,910
The one thing I would want software engineers to remember is that accessibility is not optional.

489
00:47:51,910 --> 00:47:55,910
It is not a nice extra and it is not something you add only if there is time at the end.

490
00:47:55,910 --> 00:47:57,510
It is part of whether the software works.

491
00:47:57,520 --> 00:48:09,510
Accessibility is affected by the controls you choose, the APIs you expose, the assumptions you make about input, timing, focus, error recovery, and how a user completes a real task.

492
00:48:09,510 --> 00:48:15,510
If you build the product, assuming everyone uses it like you do, you will exclude people without meaning to.

493
00:48:15,510 --> 00:48:24,150
But if you build with accessibility in mind from the start, you usually end up with software that is clearer, more robust, and easier for everyone to use.

494
00:48:24,160 --> 00:48:27,750
Was there anything we missed that you'd like to mention?

495
00:48:27,760 --> 00:48:32,309
I would emphasize that accessibility is not a niche issue.

496
00:48:32,309 --> 00:48:39,829
Everyone's ability changes over time and accessible software is usually better software for everyone.

497
00:48:39,829 --> 00:48:43,589
Also, disabled people should not only be users at the end of the process.

498
00:48:43,589 --> 00:48:48,630
We should be involved in design, engineering, testing, and decision-m.

499
00:48:50,790 --> 00:48:55,910
What apps do you recommend people look at for a great accessibility experience?

500
00:48:58,230 --> 00:49:00,470
No app is perfect, and I think that is important to say.

501
00:49:00,470 --> 00:49:10,230
I would look at the built-in accessibility systems on iOS, Android, and Windows first because they show how much can be done at the platform level, but they all have trade-offs.

502
00:49:10,230 --> 00:49:20,390
I would also look at specialist tools such as AAC apps, screen readers, switch access tools, and apps that support multiple input methods.

503
00:49:20,390 --> 00:49:22,870
Well, the best examples are not perfect apps.

504
00:49:22,870 --> 00:49:30,150
They are apps where you can see real thought about how different users complete real tasks without being forced into one interaction style.

505
00:49:30,160 --> 00:49:32,870
Excellent.

506
00:49:32,870 --> 00:49:36,150
So if people want to find out more, how can they get in touch or reach out?

507
00:49:36,160 --> 00:49:43,589
People can visit my website at owengeear.com or learn more about Switchify at switchifyapp.com.

508
00:49:43,599 --> 00:49:51,910
For Switchify specifically, the website has contact details, links to the source code, and ways to suggest features.

509
00:49:51,920 --> 00:49:53,829
Owen, thank you for coming on the show.

510
00:49:53,839 --> 00:49:55,589
It's been a real pleasure.

511
00:49:55,589 --> 00:49:57,829
This is Gavin Henry for Software Engineering Radio.

512
00:49:57,839 --> 00:50:00,950
Thank you for listening.

513
00:50:00,960 --> 00:50:03,670
Thanks again, Gavin.

514
00:50:03,670 --> 00:50:06,309
I really appreciate the chance to talk about this.

515
00:50:06,309 --> 00:50:13,510
If listeners take one thing away, I hope it is that accessibility is not only about passing a checklist.

516
00:50:13,510 --> 00:50:23,504
It is about giving people a fair chance to use software in the way that works for them.

